oner's dilemma with EDT, where two twin copies of the same model reason alike, so their choices are highly correlated. Working out the expected disutility of cooperating and defecting from the payoffs, and starting to set up the defect calculation.\n\nWorking through the cooperate-versus-defect comparison, I get the threshold q > 5/7 once I assume symmetric correlation. Since the twins share the same model and reasoning, I'm treating their choices as strongly linked despite the differing names and lead text, so cooperating comes out ahead. This is a fictional scenario, so I'll pass.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9128,"completion_tokens":711,"total_tokens":9839,"cost":0.050732,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.050732,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.036512,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.01422},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":370,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}